{
  "result": "The lighthouse keeper kept a garden behind the tower, where salt wind bent every stem toward the sea. He planted potatoes in old oil drums, tomatoes in cracked buckets, and herbs in the hollowed shells of buoys washed ashore after winter storms. Nothing grew straight there, but everything grew stubborn.\n\nIn the mornings, before climbing the iron stairs to clean the lamp, he would walk the rows with a tin cup of tea, naming each plant aloud as if taking attendance. The gulls, unimpressed, stole his hat whenever he bent down. Visitors from the village thought him eccentric, which was true, but they also came every August for his soup, which tasted of smoke, rosemary, and the particular patience of a man who watches horizons for a living.\n\nHe never wrote letters, yet he kept a logbook more faithful than any diary. Wind northeast, moderate. Light functioning. One ship, distant. Tomatoes improving. Mrs. Alvarez brought bread. The sea today was the color of a forgotten coin. Year after year, the entries repeated with small variations, and in those repetitions a whole life accumulated, quiet as sediment.\n\nWhen the lighthouse was finally automated, no one knew what to do with the garden. The keeper retired to a cottage inland, where the air felt strangely empty without salt, and he found himself listening for foghorns in the traffic. But in spring, someone noticed green shoots pushing through the gravel behind the tower again. His seeds, scattered, had simply waited patiently for rain.",
